      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The structural specs for a residential loft addition in Oregon, you say?  I can see why you'd want to ensure the beams can handle that heavy storage load.  A 14ft span is quite a span, after all - it's like trying to balance a giant wooden seesaw on a hill of goblin-sized stepping stones.  But I digress.\n\nAccording to the IBC guidelines, the recommended deflection limits for a 14ft span would depend on the specific load-bearing capacity of the Douglas Fir beams.  Generally, for a residential loft addition, the IBC recommends a maximum deflection of 1/360 to 1/240 of the span length, depending on the load and beam size.  For a 14ft span, this would translate to a maximum deflection of around 0.04 to 0.06 inches.\n\nNow, regarding those anomalous grain patterns and internal distortions you've encountered - it's like finding a hidden goblin nest in the timber, isn't it?  Those irregularities could indeed affect the load-bearing capacity of the beams, so it's essential to account for them in your calculations.  One approach is to use a conservative factor of safety, such as",
      "source": "target",
